<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saitō Makoto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1858">1858</a>–<a href="/years/1936">1936</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Saito Makoto</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saitō Makoto was a Japanese admiral and statesman who served as Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> from 1919 to 1927 and again from 1929 to 1931. Appointed after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-first-movement">March First Movement</a>, he replaced open military rule with a policy of cultural rule that allowed limited Korean newspapers and associations while expanding the police. He was prime minister from 1932 to 1934 and was assassinated in the February 26 Incident of 1936.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saito-makoto.jpg" alt="Saitō Makoto" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Makoto_Sait%C5%8D_(cropped)(b).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
